{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792918943,"nonce":"GKTNTxEIrPWhQU0GSQqy/nXBGBI=","message":"Measured at 5dbc8673 on the Mac (release, llvm-backend, ZYPY_LLVM=1, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY=1, -n 5):\n\n- deltablue iterations 40 / 13 / 0.6 / 0.4 / 0.4 ms. Blocking first-call compiles: 56 optimised (34.8 ms) + 14 quick baselines (5.4 ms), about the whole first iteration. 54 of the 56 had their body already optimised by the worker (\"ready\"), so what blocks is Cranelift codegen (about 31 ms), not the HIR pipeline. The warm-up thread meanwhile compiled 60 functions (55.1 ms, 34 of them zb_*).\n- meteor-contest iterations 87 / 41 / 63 / 42 / 38 ms. Blocking first-call compiles: 51 (about 66 ms; 17 \"loops, ready\" 25.9 ms, 9 \"loops\" 28.2 ms of which 15.5 body, 20 finished library 10.5 ms). The warm-up thread: 190 compiles, 129 ms (111 finished library functions, 58.5 ms).\n\nRayzor reached the same point differently (rayzor e0eca125, 42a38665, fac505d9, 45962e68): it starts interpreted with the native representation, calls from compiled code into an interpreted function go through a trampoline and a forwarding table instead of waiting, and its tier-up compile translates every function serially, generates machine code on all cores (rayon), then installs it in order. Nothing on the program thread waits for codegen.\n\nWhat follows for here, in order:\n1. More than one compile worker. The Cranelift compile already runs outside the backend lock (beadie_adapter.rs compile), so N workers draining CompileQueue run in parallel; `busy` becomes a count of idle workers. A body the worker optimises for a function that has been called is compiled by it too, instead of waiting for the first call from native code.\n2. A stub whose callee's compile is in flight on a worker waits for that one instead of starting its own (already so through the published table); one not in flight could enter the interpreter for this call while a worker compiles, as Rayzor's trampolines do. Worth it only where the interpreter's cost for that call is below the codegen wait.\n3. Batch installs: each compile finalizes the JIT module under the lock; installing a batch and finalizing once is what Rayzor does.","files":null}]}